## criterion bench pattern for search_entries with inline verification

When benchmarking `search_entries` (which opens threads for verification), build the DB **once** before `b.iter()` — not inside it. Reconstruction cost dominates otherwise.

```rust
fn bench_kb_search_with_verification(c: &mut Criterion) {
    let (conn, _dir) = build_bench_db();   // outside iter
    let embedder = NoopEmbedder;
    let opts = SearchOptions { inline_verify_k: 10, do_semantic: false, .. };

    c.bench_function("bench_kb_search_with_verification", |b| {
        b.iter(|| {
            criterion::black_box(
                search_entries(&conn, &embedder, "bench test", &opts).unwrap()
            );
        });
    });
}

criterion_group! {
    name = verification_benches;
    config = Criterion::default().sample_size(10);  // keeps wall time < 1min
    targets = bench_kb_search_with_verification
}
criterion_main!(verification_benches);
```

Key points:
- Use `NoopEmbedder` — `is_noop()` returns `true`, skips semantic path, no HTTP I/O.
- Pre-compute `citation_hash` with `python3 -c "import hashlib; d=open('Cargo.toml','rb').read(); print(hashlib.sha256(d[0:200]).hexdigest())"` and assert at bench start.
- `sample_size(10)` keeps the run to ~5s + warmup vs the default 100 samples.
- `do_semantic: false` explicit — even though NoopEmbedder skips, makes intent clear.

Measured result (N=50 entries, inline_verify_k=10, optimized build):
- p50: ~973 µs
- p95: ~1.02 ms
- p99: ~1.15 ms

Well under 50ms AC25 threshold → `inline_verify_k = 10` default confirmed.

## Problem
`crate::application::APP.config()` panics with "Abscissa application state accessed before it has been initialized!" when called in unit tests. Tests invoke handler functions directly, bypassing the MCP entrypoint where `APP` is initialized.

## Fix
Pass config values as parameters to handler functions. Read from `APP.config()` only at the dispatch site (the match arm in `handle_request`), where the MCP run context guarantees initialization. Tests pass `Default::default()` directly.

## Pattern
```rust
// dispatch site — APP initialized here
"compact" => {
    let vacuum_cfg = APP.config().vacuum.clone().unwrap_or_default();
    handle_compact(&id, paths, &vacuum_cfg)
}
// handler — testable without APP
fn handle_compact(id: &Value, paths: &Paths, vacuum_cfg: &VacuumConfig) -> Value { ... }
// test
let resp = handle_compact(&id, &paths, &Default::default());
```

