## FTS5 dual-write + deprecation: v1 writes must be no-ops before the DROP

When using SQLite FTS5 dual-write (contentless v1 + content='entries' v2) with an event-gated DROP, the v1 writes in apply_event must be changed to `let _ = conn.execute(...)` BEFORE the DROP lands in the same PR.

**What breaks:** If apply_event still uses `?` propagation on v1 FTS writes when the DROP gate fires, the next apply_event call after DROP returns "no such table: entries_fts" and fails hard — breaking all KB writes.

**Root cause:** The deprecation gate check runs in ensure_schema on every DB open. If the gate signals are met, the DROP fires immediately. Any subsequent apply_event call (including the very next KB write) then hits "no such table".

**Fix:** Change all v1 FTS execute calls from `?` to `let _ =`:
```rust
// Upsert arm
let _ = conn.execute("DELETE FROM entries_fts WHERE id=?1", params![id]);
let _ = conn.execute("INSERT INTO entries_fts(...) VALUES(...)", params![...]);

// Expire arm (inside BEGIN/COMMIT)
let _ = conn.execute("DELETE FROM entries_fts WHERE id=?1", params![id]);
```

**Regression test:** After setting all gate signals and calling maybe_drop_contentless_fts, call apply_event for both upsert and expire and assert Ok(). The initial deprecation gate test only verified DROP+VACUUM, not post-drop write safety.

**Evidence:** Bug caught in code review (code-reviewer, second pass via post-impl Block B). The test `test_deprecation_gate_drops_contentless_fts_when_all_signals_met` was extended with post-drop upsert/expire assertions after the fix.

## FTS5 divergence detection: BTreeSet, not Vec

When comparing two FTS5 result sets for divergence (e.g. comparing contentless vs content='entries' read paths), use `BTreeSet<&str>` for the ID comparison — not `Vec<&str>`.

**What breaks:** Vec comparison is order-sensitive. FTS5 rank ties are broken by internal storage order, which can differ between the two table implementations even when the result sets are semantically identical. This causes spurious `debug_assert_eq!` panics in debug builds on queries with tied BM25 scores.

**Fix:**
```rust
let primary_ids: std::collections::BTreeSet<&str> =
    rows.iter().map(|(id, ..)| id.as_str()).collect();
let alt_ids: std::collections::BTreeSet<&str> =
    alt.iter().map(|(id, ..)| id.as_str()).collect();
debug_assert_eq!(primary_ids, alt_ids, "fts5_dual_write_divergence: ...");
```

The release build should use the same BTreeSet pattern to keep debug/release semantics consistent.

